1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,120
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:18,800
Greetings to you from Bryce E. Cochran's book, Be Prepared, The Life and Illusions of a Scoutmaster.

3
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,520
He wrote this in 1952.

4
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:25,860
He says that the songs of the Boy Scouts of America are full of insistent tributes to the joy of hiking.

5
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:29,120
Hey, just like the Happy Wanderer, we use it as our theme song, right?

6
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:30,580
He goes on to say,

7
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:37,020
The man in charge of a group of boy hikers has somewhat the same problems that faced Moses in the managing of the Exodus,

8
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:39,580
or Napoleon in supervising the retreat from Moscow.

9
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:43,120
There's a similar effort involved in keeping up morale and discipline.

10
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:49,620
There's the same need to dispel the almost universal fear of death and thirst or privation.

11
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:55,520
There are those brave figures who collapse by the side of the road and gasp,

12
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:58,480
Go on without me. I can't make it.

13
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:03,239
Hey, this is podcast number nine.

14
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:11,280
Hey!

15
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:11,520
Hey!

16
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:37,860
Hi, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

17
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:38,780
It's Clark Green.

18
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:46,039
In Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less, this time around we're going to talk a little bit about second chances in scouting.

19
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:55,660
And I have a little story that I swiped from one of my favorite authors that I think will entertain you about his experience in scouts.

20
00:01:55,660 --> 00:02:07,280
Then three booklets, three things that will help you as a scout leader, whether you're a scoutmaster or a den leader or whatever.

21
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,780
And then we're going to wind up with a scoutmaster's minute.

22
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:15,360
And that's going to be our podcast this time.

23
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:23,080
Listen, I want to tell you about Scoutmaster Jerry and his blog and podcast, which is called the Scoutmaster's Minute.

24
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:25,420
Do go over and check him out.

25
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:30,080
I'll link it on my blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

26
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:36,620
So do go over and say hi to Jerry and take advantage of all the work that he's done over there.

27
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:37,600
Some really good stuff.

28
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:40,460
Well, without further ado, let's get started, shall we?

29
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:45,360
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

30
00:02:45,360 --> 00:03:06,800
What's the best thing that really piques my curiosity is recent brain science and research that kind of opens a door on a lot of adolescent behavior and helps to explain it.

31
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:17,960
Apparently the brain, the physical organ in our heads, is developing in adolescence that we really had not known before.

32
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:24,920
So there's a couple of broad concepts that I draw from that that help inform my work as a scoutmaster.

33
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:33,200
One is that when adolescents are thinking, which might be an exclusion in terms,

34
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:43,640
but when adolescents are thinking and making plans and trying to weigh risks and the advisability of different courses of action,

35
00:03:44,179 --> 00:03:48,760
they're working with a physically limited amount of brain matter.

36
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:54,540
I mean, actual physical limitation.

37
00:03:54,980 --> 00:04:03,100
It's not that there's a mature, reasonable adult sitting inside their head and they're listening to that and saying,

38
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,320
Nah, I'm not going to do that.

39
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:12,899
I'm going to go for the really risky, stupid idea, you know, which is what happens in adolescence sometimes.

40
00:04:13,859 --> 00:04:20,240
I'm going to react emotionally instead of cognitively to this situation.

41
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:29,000
They're not really making that choice as much as going along with the dictates of the actual physical function of their brain.

42
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:31,060
Is that making sense to you?

43
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:43,760
This does not mean that we divorce any consequences for action, no matter whether it is just based on, you know, brain science or not.

44
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:51,960
Because adolescence is also that period of time in which we learn about consequences and responsibility and things like that.

45
00:04:51,960 --> 00:05:01,739
But what it does do for me as a scoutmaster and as somebody who works with youth is it informs my attitude.

46
00:05:02,239 --> 00:05:11,500
It informs my attitude about the way that we should deal with, you know, the miscreants that we run across sometime in scouting.

47
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:33,920
Now, with over two decades of time as a scoutmaster, I will tell you that I have had scouts who have gotten in fistfights, who have been caught in lies, who have been caught stealing, who have been caught smoking, who have made bad decisions in one way or another.

48
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:37,500
And I've even had a couple of them charged with crimes.

49
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:40,940
But I will tell you something.

50
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:47,540
In this two plus decades of scouting, I have never kicked one out of my scout troop.

51
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:48,360
Never.

52
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:57,360
I think that when you're in this age, you're particularly vulnerable to making bad decisions.

53
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:07,300
You need to have at least one person in the world or one group in the world that believes enough in you to give you a second chance.

54
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:17,080
We do have a judicial system, as do most countries in the world, a judicial system that is different for minors than it is for adults.

55
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:30,740
We have that system because we understand that there are questions of accountability in the mind and the actions of a minor that are not the same in somebody who is an adult.

56
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:44,620
There are questions of reason and the ability of them to overcome outside influences that we ascribe to adults that we do not ascribe to minors.

57
00:06:44,620 --> 00:07:00,300
I have to think that it is both wise and proper for us as scout leaders to look for opportunities not to kick guys out of a scouting experience,

58
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:07,040
but to include them in it and to give them as much of a second chance as possible.

59
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:22,200
This all comes to mind to me because recently I sat down with a parent and a scout who had been asked not to come back to a troop across town.

60
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:32,500
I am not going to go into lengthy explanations and details about the incident that led up to this,

61
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:42,640
but suffice to say this was one incident and it was not a pattern of behavior so far as I could tell.

62
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:55,200
So, you know, I asked some questions about school and family and was satisfied that this fella is not a risk to the safety of my other scouts

63
00:07:55,200 --> 00:08:01,060
and is not going to be an undue poor influence.

64
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:09,500
Another important piece of evidence is that this fella has been in a scout troop for about four years.

65
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:11,600
He was almost done with life when he left.

66
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:15,020
If you're a star scout, this is what's happened.

67
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:17,080
You've had four scout master conferences.

68
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:19,180
You've had four boards of review.

69
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,760
This guy had about a dozen merit badges.

70
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:26,760
So he has worked with, you know, about a dozen merit badge counselors.

71
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:32,080
He has held a position of responsibility in his troop for at least four months.

72
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:38,080
His execution of that position has been approved by his scout leaders.

73
00:08:38,939 --> 00:08:40,900
So there's a lot of evidence there.

74
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:48,340
This guy, if he came to me and he was 16 and had little or nothing in the way of rank advancement,

75
00:08:48,620 --> 00:08:55,980
if I learned that he was a problem in school and perhaps he had come to the attention of the civil authorities once or twice,

76
00:08:55,980 --> 00:09:02,640
then I would have to do some hard thinking about exactly how we would structure his return to scouting.

77
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:04,300
I have a bold prediction.

78
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:07,400
I think this guy will probably become an Eagle Scout.

79
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:10,300
I think this is what he wants to do.

80
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:16,800
I think he deeply regrets the actions that he took that led him to not be included in his troop.

81
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:21,120
But this all begs one more question.

82
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:28,300
Why couldn't his home troop, the troop that he was with for four years, try and figure this out?

83
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:32,240
And try and make good on the promise to this guy.

84
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:35,300
And try and realize the potential that he had.

85
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,160
That's going to remain a mystery because I'm not going any further with it.

86
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:44,780
But it's a question that I think is worth pondering for us scoutmasters.

87
00:09:44,780 --> 00:09:48,600
Have you handed out second chances that have borne fruit?

88
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:49,880
Because I know I have.

89
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:54,260
Remember I talked about guys who have basically run the gamut of bad behavior,

90
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:58,079
who have come to the attention of the civil authorities for one reason or another?

91
00:09:58,579 --> 00:10:04,060
Well, I remember these guys chiefly because they went on to become Eagle Scouts.

92
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:07,860
Scouting is about second, third, fourth, fifth chances.

93
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:15,520
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

94
00:10:18,820 --> 00:10:22,380
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

95
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:29,320
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

96
00:10:29,560 --> 00:10:32,700
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

97
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:36,120
You were always on the beat, boy, beat, boy.

98
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:39,580
Yeah, you were always on the beat, boy, beat, boy.

99
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:43,040
I'm hanging in the street, boy, street, boy.

100
00:10:43,480 --> 00:10:46,380
He renouncing to the beat, boy, beat, boy.

101
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:49,780
Yeah, you're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

102
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:53,280
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

103
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:57,480
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

104
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:06,180
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

105
00:11:09,620 --> 00:11:13,040
Garrison Keillor, the host of A Prairie Home Companion and Arthur,

106
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:17,000
wrote this about his days in scouting in Lake Wobegon in his book,

107
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:20,440
Lake Wobegon Days, talking about his scoutmaster, Einar Tingvold.

108
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:30,380
Why did we need to know semaphore code?

109
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:35,040
Well, Einar said it was handy for sending messages in the outdoors at a distance of up to a half a mile.

110
00:11:36,060 --> 00:11:41,200
Imagine, he said, if you're camping on a hill and another troop is on another hill a half mile away

111
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,360
and suddenly you need medical help.

112
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:47,000
Well, you flash a mirror at the other camp and you get their attention,

113
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,020
and they train their binoculars on your camp,

114
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:53,140
and meanwhile you take two shirts and tie them to two sticks,

115
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:56,120
and now you're ready to send a message using semaphore code.

116
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,980
This is why we need to learn this.

117
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:11,700
Imagine you were sending an urgent message somewhere and somebody couldn't read it.

118
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:14,200
Why, help could be delayed for hours.

119
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:16,160
Somebody could die as a result.

120
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:19,959
Einar's only answer raised a lot of questions in my mind.

121
00:12:20,140 --> 00:12:21,560
I mean, number one is what hills.

122
00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:28,219
You need to have pretty high hills to be able to see a fellow scout waving his flags a half a mile away,

123
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:29,680
even with binoculars.

124
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:32,060
We don't have any hills like that in the out there.

125
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:42,819
Number two, what binoculars?

126
00:12:42,939 --> 00:12:44,160
None of us scouts had a pair.

127
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:48,300
Now, Einar had one, but what good would that do if he was with us,

128
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:50,260
the troop that needed urgent medical help?

129
00:12:50,459 --> 00:12:54,900
All he could do with his binoculars would be to see that the other troop couldn't see us.

130
00:13:03,020 --> 00:13:05,040
Number three, what other troop?

131
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:10,719
No other scout troop camped around where we camped out on the Talaroods pasture with its one extremely low hill.

132
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:16,680
The only people to see our semaphore signals would be the Talaroods, and probably they'd just think we were some kind of kids waving shirts.

133
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:29,579
Number four, if we needed urgent help, why not just get into Einar's car and drive to the doctor's?

134
00:13:30,439 --> 00:13:32,400
Einar always had his car when we went camping.

135
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:33,540
I mean, that's how we got there.

136
00:13:33,979 --> 00:13:41,839
Why stand around waving at a non-existent troop on a hill that wasn't high enough and send probably a misspelled message in the process?

137
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:44,680
Unjent, send heave, I'm Bradley Kurt.

138
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:47,980
Alls we really had to do was hop into Einar's studio, baby.

139
00:13:47,980 --> 00:13:50,720
Mulleroo!

140
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:54,160
Woo!

141
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:55,940
Maywe!

142
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:15,780
Mooreend

143
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:28,980
Now, nine out of ten problems you deal with as a scout leader are not coming from the scouts themselves.

144
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:36,420
They're coming from some kind of need to find a policy somewhere or a question that you might come up with

145
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:39,560
or even an argument you might get into with a fellow leader.

146
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,100
As civil as these arguments may be, sometimes they can really stymie the program.

147
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:45,940
Here's an example.

148
00:14:46,300 --> 00:14:50,680
Boy's getting ready to do his Eagle Project, and the committee chairman says,

149
00:14:51,040 --> 00:14:53,980
oh, scouts aren't allowed to use power tools or climb ladders.

150
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:58,680
That kind of throws a big old monkey wrench into everything, and we need to go looking for answers.

151
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:02,100
Now, where are we going to find those answers?

152
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:04,440
Ah, well, there's a very simple rule.

153
00:15:05,180 --> 00:15:10,260
Yule Brenner, probably best personified it in Cecil B. DeMille's The Ten Commandments.

154
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:11,080
So let it be written.

155
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:13,520
So let it be done.

156
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:15,040
So let it be written.

157
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:16,240
So let it be done.

158
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:19,960
So it's written down, and it's written down in the guide to safe scouting.

159
00:15:20,140 --> 00:15:21,500
Do you have the guide to safe scouting?

160
00:15:21,860 --> 00:15:28,060
It's now available as a PDF file that I will link to on the blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com,

161
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:31,780
and it will answer most of the common sticky questions that you have.

162
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:33,199
So what about tools?

163
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:34,199
What about power tools?

164
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,380
And what about ladders?

165
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:38,800
The guide is kind of silent about ladders.

166
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:43,479
Now, climbing a ladder is a fairly ubiquitous and common activity, so if there was a prohibition

167
00:15:43,479 --> 00:15:47,160
against it, I think the guide to safe scouting would say something about it.

168
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:48,500
I'm certain that it would.

169
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:54,599
As to power tools, there's a handy chart called age-appropriate activities that indicates

170
00:15:54,599 --> 00:16:00,520
that scouts, in other words, boys who are members of a Boy Scout troop, that means between

171
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:04,099
the ages of 10 1⁄2 and 18, are permitted to use power tools.

172
00:16:04,100 --> 00:16:08,640
You would have to hunt a little bit, but you would find a prohibition against using chainsaws

173
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:10,120
and mechanical log splitters.

174
00:16:10,340 --> 00:16:12,780
But you're going to hunt a little bit, because you're going to learn the book.

175
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:17,240
You don't have to know chapter and verse in it, but you just need to know that it exists,

176
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:19,340
and that it's a wonderful reference for you.

177
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:23,560
How about let's go to a paintball place and make it a troop outing and do paintball?

178
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:25,620
No, sorry.

179
00:16:26,580 --> 00:16:28,060
You really can't do that.

180
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:34,439
Specifically, on page 39, it says, pointing any type of firearm, including paintball, dye,

181
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:36,719
or lasers, at any individual is unauthorized.

182
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:38,300
It's just an unauthorized activity.

183
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:41,400
It's not something that scouts do, so we don't do it.

184
00:16:42,060 --> 00:16:48,199
If you're in a law enforcement venturing program, then there's an exception.

185
00:16:48,859 --> 00:16:50,920
But as for Boy Scouts, no.

186
00:16:51,079 --> 00:16:52,119
We don't do paintball.

187
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:53,959
We don't do laser tag.

188
00:16:54,040 --> 00:16:54,939
We don't do any of that.

189
00:16:55,079 --> 00:16:56,599
It's an unauthorized activity.

190
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:00,040
Again, the guide to safe scouting, it's available as a PDF.

191
00:17:00,280 --> 00:17:02,920
It'll be on scoutmastertypepad.com.

192
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:10,420
So there's another real handy guide that you should have, at least in the PDF file or in a hard copy,

193
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:15,220
ready to pull out and to use for references,

194
00:17:15,420 --> 00:17:18,980
because the advancement system of scouting is pretty direct and simple,

195
00:17:19,260 --> 00:17:21,800
but there are times when you really need some help,

196
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:25,140
where you really need to know some things.

197
00:17:25,140 --> 00:17:29,840
For instance, a very common thing that is said is that, you know,

198
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:34,960
if you're working on a merit badge, the requirements for the merit badge expire in six months.

199
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:37,220
If you don't finish it, if you don't finish a partial,

200
00:17:37,700 --> 00:17:40,300
and you let it go for six months or a year or whatever,

201
00:17:40,820 --> 00:17:42,700
then you have to begin all over again.

202
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:49,720
Now, that sounds, at the same time, like a smart rule and a stupid rule for a lot of reasons.

203
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:54,600
But what is the actual national policy on merit badges?

204
00:17:54,740 --> 00:17:59,380
Well, it's right there in the Advancement Committee Guidebook.

205
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:00,520
That's what it's called.

206
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:06,020
It's called the Advancement Committee Policies and Procedures Book.

207
00:18:06,140 --> 00:18:07,340
It's available as a PDF.

208
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:11,560
Again, it'll be at the blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com,

209
00:18:11,639 --> 00:18:15,080
and it will save you a lot of wear and tear,

210
00:18:15,679 --> 00:18:21,000
because you'll find the answer to the question that I just asked about, you know,

211
00:18:21,060 --> 00:18:22,980
when do requirements for a merit badge expire,

212
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:26,679
and they say there's no expiry date except the Scout's 18th birthday.

